==English==
[[File:Clarias batrachus.jpg|thumb|A [[walking catfish]]]]
[[File:Platydoras costatus 2 (Piotr Kuczynski).jpg|thumb|A [[w:talking catfish|talking catfish]]]]

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/ˈkætfɪʃ/|[ˈkʰæt̚fɪʃ]}}
* {{audio|en|En-au-catfish.ogg|a=AU}}

===Etymology 1===
From {{compound|en|cat|fish}}. Likely so named for its prominent [[barbel]]s like a [[cat#English|cat]]'s [[whisker]]s. Compare {{cog|fy|katfisk|t=catfish}}, {{cog|nl|katvis|t=catfish}}. Compare also {{cog|de|Seekatze|t=catfish|lit=sea-cat}}.

====Noun====
{{en-noun|~|catfish|+}}

# {{senseid|en|Q59576}}Any fish of the order {{taxfmt|Siluriformes|order}}, mainly found in [[freshwater|fresh water]], lacking [[scale]]s, and having [[barbel]]s like [[whisker]]s around the mouth.
#: {{syn|en|catlet|q1=Africa|sheat|sheatfish|river chicken}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2013|author={{w|Ruth Ozeki}}|title=''{{w|A Tale for the Time Being}}''|publisher=Canongate Books (2019)|page=213|passage=“You don’t see '''catfish''' that big anymore, except in Chernobyl {{...}} Nobody fishes there anymore, so the '''catfish''' thrive. They’ve gotten really enormous, some even twelve or thirteen feet long. They’re bottom-feeders, and apparently the mud still contains a lot of radioactive particles, but the '''catfish''' don’t seem to mind.”}}
# The meat of such a fish, popular in the Southern U.S. and Central Europe.

